---
import AdSlot from '../components/AdSlot.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import {faqs} from '../lib/faqs.mjs';
import {SPECIAL_ANGLES} from '../lib/diagrams.mjs';
import {exactTrig,radiansLabel} from '../lib/math.mjs';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';import UnitCircle from '../components/UnitCircle.astro';
const quadrant=a=>a%90===0?'Axis':['I','II','III','IV'][Math.floor(a/90)];
---
<Layout title="Interactive Unit Circle With Tangent: Chart & Values" headline="Interactive unit circle" section="Unit circle" description="Drag around an interactive unit circle to see sine, cosine, and tangent, then print the full unit circle chart with exact values for every special angle."><Breadcrumbs items={[['Tools','/tools/'],['Unit circle','/unit-circle/']]}/><div class="intro"><div><div class="eyebrow">Trigonometry, in motion · every angle has a story</div><h1>Interactive unit circle with tangent</h1><p>Follow a point around the circle. Its coordinates are (cos θ, sin θ), and tan θ = sin θ / cos θ. Below the explorer is a complete unit circle chart with exact values.</p></div></div><UnitCircle/><AdSlot type="rectangle"/>
<section class="explanation" id="unit-circle-chart"><h2>Unit circle chart with sine, cosine & tangent</h2><p>Every special angle from 0° to 330°, in degrees and radians, with its point (cos θ, sin θ) and tangent. Tangent is undefined at 90° and 270°, where cos θ = 0.</p><figure><img src="/unit-circle-chart.svg" width="900" height="900" loading="lazy" alt="Unit circle chart labeled with degrees, radians, coordinates (cos θ, sin θ), and tan θ for all 16 special angles" style="width:100%;max-width:720px;height:auto;display:block;margin:0 auto"/><figcaption class="small-note" style="text-align:center"><a class="text-link" href="/unit-circle-chart.svg" download="unit-circle-chart.svg">Download the printable unit circle chart (SVG) ↗</a></figcaption></figure>
<div class="fact-table-wrap"><table class="fact-table chart-table"><caption class="small-note" style="caption-side:top;text-align:left">Exact values on the unit circle</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Degrees</th><th scope="col">Radians</th><th scope="col">cos θ</th><th scope="col">sin θ</th><th scope="col">tan θ</th><th scope="col">Quadrant</th></tr></thead><tbody>{SPECIAL_ANGLES.map(a=><tr><td>{a}°</td><td>{radiansLabel(a)}</td><td>{exactTrig(a,'cos')}</td><td>{exactTrig(a,'sin')}</td><td>{exactTrig(a,'tan')}</td><td>{quadrant(a)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
<h3>How to remember the chart</h3><ul><li>In quadrant I, the sines of 30°, 45°, and 60° are √1/2 (= ½), √2/2, and √3/2; the cosines run the same list backwards.</li><li>Signs follow the quadrant: “All Students Take Calculus” means all three ratios are positive in I, only sine in II, only tangent in III, and only cosine in IV.</li><li>Each angle in quadrants II–IV reuses a quadrant-I value: 150° has the same reference angle, 30°, as 210° and 330°.</li></ul></section>
<div class="related"><a href="/learn/radians-and-degrees/">Radians to degrees chart ↗</a><a href="/learn/trigonometric-ratios/">Sine, cosine & tangent ↗</a><a href="/tools/trig-graphs/">Sine & cosine graphs ↗</a><a href="/practice/">Practice special angles ↗</a></div><AdSlot type="native"/><Faq items={faqs['unit-circle']} title="Unit circle questions"/></Layout>
